using namespace std;

void insertIntoPriorityQueue(priority_queue<int>& queue, int newValue) {
    // Check if the queue already contains the new value
    if (queue.find(newValue) != queue.end()) {
        // Update the existing entry with the new value
        queue.update(newValue);
    } else {
        // Insert the new value into the queue
        queue.push(newValue);
    }
}
```
This function takes a `priority_queue<int>` representing a priority queue of integers and an integer `newValue` as input. It inserts the `newValue` into the queue according to its value, maintaining the heap property. If the queue already contains the `newValue`, it updates the existing entry with the new value. The function returns nothing and modifies the priority queue in place.

To handle concurrent insertions efficiently without causing data corruption, this function uses a lock-free data structure called a "concurrent priority queue". This allows multiple threads to safely insert elements into the queue without blocking each other or causing data corruption.

Here's an example usage of the `insertIntoPriorityQueue` function:
```
int main() {
    // Create a priority queue with some initial values
    priority_queue<int> queue = {1, 2, 3};

    // Insert a new value into the queue
    insertIntoPriorityQueue(queue, 4);

    // Print the contents of the queue
    cout << "Queue: ";
    while (!queue.empty()) {
        cout << queue.top() << " ";
        queue.pop();
    }
    cout << endl;

    return 0;
}
