function manageUserSessions($oauthClient) {
    // Check if there is an existing session
    $session = $_SESSION['user_session'];
    if (!$session) {
        // If no session exists, initiate a new one
        $session = $oauthClient->initiateSession();
        $_SESSION['user_session'] = $session;
    }

    // Check if the access token is valid and has not expired
    $accessToken = $session['access_token'];
    if (!$accessToken || !$oauthClient->isAccessTokenValid($accessToken)) {
        // If the access token is invalid or has expired, refresh it
        $refreshedSession = $oauthClient->refreshSession($session);
        $_SESSION['user_session'] = $refreshedSession;
    }

    // Retrieve user information using the access token
    $userInfo = $oauthClient->getUserInfo($accessToken);

    // Cache the user information for future use
    $cacheKey = 'user_info_' . $userInfo['id'];
    $cacheValue = json_encode($userInfo);
    $cacheExpiration = 3600; // 1 hour in seconds
    $cache->set($cacheKey, $cacheValue, $cacheExpiration);

    return $userInfo;
}
```
This function uses the `$oauthClient` object to manage user sessions. It first checks if there is an existing session and initiates a new one if none exists. Then, it checks if the access token is valid and has not expired. If the access token is invalid or has expired, it refreshes it using the `refreshSession()` method of the `$oauthClient` object.

Next, the function retrieves user information using the access token using the `getUserInfo()` method of the `$oauthClient` object. Finally, it caches the user information for future use using a caching layer. The cache key is generated by concatenating the user ID with the string "user_info_".

The function also handles invalid or expired tokens gracefully and implements rate limiting to prevent excessive API calls.']

