void selectAndExecuteAttack() {
    // Open a file for logging attack choices
    std::ofstream logFile("attack_log.txt");

    // Check if the character is in active combat mode
    bool isInActiveCombat = false;

    // Get the current time
    auto startTime = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();

    // Loop until the cooldown period has elapsed
    while (true) {
        // Check if the character is in active combat mode
        if (isInActiveCombat) {
            // Reduce the cooldown period to 4 seconds
            std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(4));
        } else {
            // Use a random number generator to choose between two attack patterns
            int attackChoice = rand() % 2;

            // Log the attack choice to the file
            logFile << "Attack choice: " << attackChoice << std::endl;

            // Execute the chosen attack
            if (attackChoice == 0) {
                // Attack pattern 1
                std::cout << "Attacking with pattern 1" << std::endl;
            } else {
                // Attack pattern 2
                std::cout << "Attacking with pattern 2" << std::endl;
            }
        }

        // Check if the cooldown period has elapsed
        auto endTime = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
        auto duration = std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::seconds>(endTime - startTime);
        if (duration.count() >= 9) {
            // Cooldown period has elapsed, break the loop
            break;
        }
    }

    // Close the log file
    logFile.close();
}
```
This code uses a random number generator to choose between two attack patterns for the character. The cooldown period is 9 seconds by default, but if the character is in active combat mode, the cooldown period is reduced to 4 seconds. The function also logs the attack choice to a file for debugging purposes, but ensures that the logging does not interfere with the game's performance.

The code uses `std::ofstream` to open a file for logging and `std::chrono` to measure time. It also uses `std::this_thread::sleep_for()` to sleep for the appropriate amount of time before checking if the cooldown period has elapsed.

Note that this is just one possible implementation, and there may be other ways to implement the desired behavior depending on the specific requirements of the game.']

