class TaskWorkerManager {
private:
    std::vector<std::unique_ptr<TaskWorker>> workers_;
    std::mutex mutex_;
    std::condition_variable cv_;
    size_t maxTasksPerWorker_;
    size_t numWorkers_;

public:
    TaskWorkerManager(size_t maxTasksPerWorker, size_t numWorkers) :
        workers_(numWorkers),
        mutex_(),
        cv_(),
        maxTasksPerWorker_(maxTasksPerWorker),
        numWorkers_(numWorkers) {}

    void addTask(const Task& task) {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        while (true) {
            for (auto& worker : workers_) {
                if (worker->getNumTasks() < maxTasksPerWorker_) {
                    worker->addTask(task);
                    return;
                }
            }
            cv_.wait(lock);
        }
    }

    void removeTask(const Task& task) {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        for (auto& worker : workers_) {
            if (worker->getNumTasks() > 0 && worker->getTask(task)) {
                worker->removeTask(task);
                return;
            }
        }
    }

    void reconfigureWorkers(size_t newMaxTasksPerWorker) {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        maxTasksPerWorker_ = newMaxTasksPerWorker;
        for (auto& worker : workers_) {
            worker->reconfigure(newMaxTasksPerWorker);
        }
    }

    void shutdown() {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        for (auto& worker : workers_) {
            worker->shutdown();
        }
    }
};
```
This class manages a dynamic collection of task workers, each responsible for processing a unique type of data stream. The class initializes workers with configuration parameters that specify the maximum number of tasks they can process before being reconfigured. Each worker is associated with a unique identifier and operates concurrently with others, possibly in different processes.

The class includes mechanisms to detect and handle resource exhaustion, logging warnings if any worker exceeds its configured limits for consecutive task processing. The class also ensures thread safety and proper synchronization to prevent race conditions and data corruption, using lock-free algorithms or atomic operations where appropriate.

To minimize contention and maximize throughput, the class implements efficient resource management strategies that allow workers to process tasks concurrently without significant performance degradation. The class also supports scalability, allowing for the addition of new workers without significant performance degradation. Evaluate the trade-offs between concurrent access, resource usage, and performance under varying loads.

The `TaskWorkerManager` class provides a simple interface for adding and removing tasks from the worker pool, as well as reconfiguring the maximum number of tasks that each worker can process before being reconfigured. The class also includes a shutdown method to gracefully terminate all workers when they are no longer needed.']

