This code implements a distributed logging system that can efficiently handle large volumes of structured and unstructured logs from multiple sources. The system uses a pool of log handlers to manage different types of log data, including error messages, performance metrics, and user activity logs. It also uses a pool of worker threads to process the logs asynchronously, allowing for optimal performance under varying loads.

The system dynamically adjusts the size of the log handler and worker thread pools based on system resources and application demand, prioritizing performance over memory efficiency when under high load. It implements mechanisms to manage memory usage and prevent leaks, ensuring optimal performance under varying loads. Additionally, it integrates support for asynchronous logging to minimize the impact on system throughput, ensuring that all operations are completed within a specified time frame to meet service-level agreements (SLAs).

The system also supports automatic failover and redundancy in case of logging node failures, allowing seamless switching to backup logging nodes without interrupting log collection. It implements a rate-limiting mechanism to control the number of log entries based on predefined policies, ensuring fair usage and preventing overwhelming of storage resources. To optimize resource utilization, the system periodically monitors system load and adjusts the number of available worker threads accordingly, balancing between responsiveness and resource consumption.

Finally, the system integrates support for distributed tracing to provide end-to-end visibility into the system's behavior, facilitating troubleshooting and performance analysis. The code is modular and extensible, allowing easy integration of new features and interfaces in the future. It also supports horizontal scaling to accommodate additional logging nodes and increased log volumes without requiring significant changes to the codebase."]

