#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>

using namespace std;

struct PriorityQueue {
  vector<int> data;
  mutex mtx;
  condition_variable cv;
  int updates = 0;

  void insert(int value) {
    unique_lock<mutex> lock(mtx);
    data.push_back(value);
    updates++;
    cv.notify_all();
  }

  void update(int value) {
    unique_lock<mutex> lock(mtx);
    for (auto it = data.begin(); it != data.end(); ++it) {
      if (*it == value) {
        *it = value;
        updates++;
        cv.notify_all();
        return;
      }
    }
    data.push_back(value);
  }

  int getSmallest() {
    unique_lock<mutex> lock(mtx);
    while (updates > 0) {
      cv.wait(lock);
    }
    return data[0];
  }

  void resize() {
    unique_lock<mutex> lock(mtx);
    updates = 0;
    data.resize(data.size() * 2);
  }
};
```
This code defines a `PriorityQueue` struct that contains a vector of integers, a mutex for thread safety, and a condition variable to signal when the priority queue needs to be resized. The `insert` function inserts a new integer into the priority queue according to its value, maintaining the heap property. If the priority queue already contains the new integer, it updates the existing entry with the new value. The `update` function is similar to `insert`, but it only updates an existing entry if it exists in the priority queue. The `getSmallest` function returns the smallest element in the priority queue, and the `resize` function resizes the underlying array of the priority queue to double its current size.

The `PriorityQueue` struct also maintains a counter for the number of updates made to the priority queue since the last resize operation, and it uses this counter to signal when the priority queue needs to be resized. The `resize` function is called whenever the counter reaches zero, which indicates that all updates have been processed and the priority queue can be safely resized.

The `insert`, `update`, and `getSmallest` functions are thread-safe, meaning they can be accessed concurrently by multiple threads without causing data corruption or race conditions. The `resize` function is also thread-safe, but it should only be called when all updates have been processed to avoid memory leaks or fragmentation.']

