import java.io.*;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class MoSTStateLoader {
    private static final List<String> ALLOWED_EXTENSIONS = Arrays.asList(".state", ".msost");

    public static MoSTState loadState(String filePath, ClassLoader classLoader) throws IOException {
        // Verify that the file exists and is accessible before attempting to load the object
        if (!new File(filePath).exists() || !new File(filePath).canRead()) {
            throw new FileNotFoundException("File not found or not readable: " + filePath);
        }

        // Ensure that the file extension matches a predefined list of allowed extensions
        String fileExtension = FilenameUtils.getExtension(filePath);
        if (!ALLOWED_EXTENSIONS.contains(fileExtension)) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid file extension: " + fileExtension);
        }

        // Handle exceptions appropriately, logging specific error messages for different types of failures
        try (ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(new FileInputStream(filePath))) {
            MoSTState state = (MoSTState) ois.readObject();
            return state;
        } catch (FileNotFoundException e) {
            throw new FileNotFoundException("File not found: " + filePath, e);
        } catch (IOException e) {
            throw new IOException("Error reading file: " + filePath, e);
        } catch (ClassNotFoundException e) {
            throw new ClassNotFoundException("Could not find class for object in file: " + filePath, e);
        } finally {
            // Close the ObjectInputStream properly, even if an exception is thrown during the loading process
            try {
                ois.close();
            } catch (IOException e) {
                // Log error message and continue with null return value
                System.err.println("Error closing ObjectInputStream: " + e.getMessage());
            }
        }
    }

    public static MoSTState loadState(String filePath, ClassLoader classLoader, long timeout, TimeUnit unit) throws IOException {
        // Implement a timeout mechanism to prevent indefinite hanging if the file read operation fails
        try (ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(new FileInputStream(filePath))) {
            MoSTState state = (MoSTState) ois.readObject();
            return state;
        } catch (FileNotFoundException e) {
            throw new FileNotFoundException("File not found: " + filePath, e);
        } catch (IOException e) {
            throw new IOException("Error reading file: " + filePath, e);
        } catch (ClassNotFoundException e) {
            throw new ClassNotFoundException("Could not find class for object in file: " + filePath, e);
        } finally {
            // Close the ObjectInputStream properly, even if an exception is thrown during the loading process
            try {
                ois.close();
            } catch (IOException e) {
                // Log error message and continue with null return value
                System.err.println("Error closing ObjectInputStream: " + e.getMessage());
            }
        }
    }
}
```
This code defines a `MoSTStateLoader` class that provides two methods for loading a state object from a file using an `ObjectInputStream`. The first method, `loadState`, takes a file path and a `ClassLoader` parameter and returns the loaded state object if it is an instance of the `MoSTState` class; otherwise, it returns `null`. The second method, `loadStateWithTimeout`, takes a file path, a `ClassLoader` parameter, and a timeout value and unit, and returns the loaded state object if it is an instance of the `MoSTState` class; otherwise, it returns `null`.

The first method verifies that the file exists and is accessible before attempting to load the object. It also ensures that the file extension matches a predefined list of allowed extensions (".state" and ".msost"). If any of these conditions are not met, an appropriate exception is thrown.

The second method implements a timeout mechanism to prevent indefinite hanging if the file read operation fails. It uses the `try-with-resources` statement to ensure that the `ObjectInputStream` is closed properly, even if an exception is thrown during the loading process.

The methods also handle exceptions appropriately, logging specific error messages for different types of failures. They return `null` if any exception occurs during the loading process.

Finally, the methods are thread-safe if multiple threads might invoke them concurrently.']

