The constructor takes an optional argument for the idle timeout period in seconds. If no value is provided, it defaults to 30 seconds.

The `addSession` method adds a new session to the manager with the given `sessionID` and `userID`. The `updateSession` method updates an existing session in the manager with the given `sessionID` and `userID`.

The `expireInactiveSessions` method expires all inactive sessions based on the idle timeout period. It iterates over all the sessions in the map, checks if each session is inactive (based on the last activity timestamp), and removes it from the map if it is.

The `getActiveSessionCount` method returns the number of active sessions in the manager. The `isSessionActive` method checks if a given session is still active based on its last activity timestamp and the idle timeout period.

This code uses an unordered map to store all the sessions, which allows for efficient lookups by session ID. It also uses chrono library to track the time and calculate the inactivity periods.

Note that this code does not handle concurrent access issues, so it is important to ensure that only one thread can modify the manager at a time. Additionally, it does not handle resource exhaustion scenarios, so it is important to monitor the memory usage of the program and adjust the idle timeout period accordingly.']

