    buf_size = lseek(input_fd, 0, SEEK_END);
    input_buf = malloc(buf_size);
    if (input_buf == NULL) {
        handle_error();
    }

    // Open the output file with write access and create a buffer for writing to it
    if (output_file_path != NULL) {
        output_fd = open_file(output_file_path, O_WRONLY | O_CREAT);
        if (output_fd == -1) {
            handle_error();
        }
        buf_size = lseek(output_fd, 0, SEEK_END);
        output_buf = malloc(buf_size);
        if (output_buf == NULL) {
            handle_error();
        }
    }

    // Read from the input file and write to the output file until the end of the file is reached
    while ((num_bytes_read = read_file(input_fd, input_buf, buf_size)) > 0) {
        if (output_file_path != NULL) {
            num_bytes_written = write_file(output_fd, output_buf, num_bytes_read);
            if (num_bytes_written == -1) {
                handle_error();
            }
        }
    }

    // Close the input and output files and free the buffers
    close_file(input_fd);
    close_file(output_fd);
    free(input_buf);
    free(output_buf);

    // Print the number of lines processed
    printf("Processed %d lines\n", line_count);

    return 0;
}
```
This program takes an input file path as a command line argument and reads from it line by line. It then writes each line to an output file, if specified, while maintaining the original directory path of the input file. The program also supports various options, such as case-sensitive or case-insensitive line counting, redirecting output to a specified file, limiting the number of lines processed, and displaying detailed usage information.

The program uses `sprintf` and `strcat` to construct the new filename based on the original filename and the random digit sequence. It also uses `access` to check if the constructed filename exists and tries up to 10 times to find a unique filename by appending a random number to the original filename until no conflict is found.

The program parses a JSON configuration file, if present, to determine the default file type and maximum length of the random digit sequence. It also handles errors gracefully by logging them without interruption and exiting with a non-zero status code.']

